{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Network Flow Control.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Network Flow Control.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"network flow control network flow control","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Network Flow Control.md","fileName":"Network Flow Control.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Network Flow Control","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-27 00:11\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[.NET Distributed Memory and Message Passing]]","tags":["baby"],"links":[".NET Distributed Memory and Message Passing","hands-onparallelprogrammingwithc8andnetcore3.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Network Flow Control.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Network Flow Control.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"network flow control network flow control","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Network Flow Control.md","fileName":"Network Flow Control.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Network Flow Control","heading":"Network Flow Control","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Network flow control prevents a sender or upstream link from delivering data faster than the receiver and intermediate buffers can absorb it. Credits, windows, or backpressure communicate available capacity through the path.\n\nWithout flow control, overload produces buffer exhaustion and loss; overly conservative control leaves links idle. In distributed computation, these effects influence whether communication overlaps useful work or stalls the participating processes.","tags":["baby"],"links":[".NET Distributed Memory and Message Passing","hands-onparallelprogrammingwithc8andnetcore3.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Network Flow Control.md::2","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Network Flow Control.md","sourceType":"markdow